# Mohana-Mukhari!
Tamil title: மோகன முகாறி!

“His musical ability—ah, it is marvellous, of an uncommon kind. Listeners are transported.”

“Is that so? What is the musician's special distinction?”

“Why do you ask? He is extraordinarily adept at raga elaboration. He has no equal.”

While two friends were speaking thus about a musician, another friend joined them.

“Whom are you discussing?” the newcomer asked. The friend who had been praising replied proudly, “Surely you know our musician Vetri Oliyar. We were speaking about him.”

The friend who had voiced doubt said wearily, “So it is Vetri Oliyar's skill in raga elaboration that he praises so highly?”

Hearing both, the newcomer laughed heartily. “Are you speaking of that new performer and praising him as unequalled in raga elaboration? Friend, it is natural under the rules of friendship that you care for Vetri Oliyar and praise his musical skill. But had you even one-tenth as much love for music as you have for your friend, you would not praise his raga elaboration.”

The praising friend stood bewildered. Just then Vetri Oliyar himself came that way and, at their request, sang proudly. The man who had mocked them told the admirer: “Sir, you understand your friend's ability, but not the rules, method and structure prescribed for ragas. That is why you can mistake his noise for music. Ask him what raga he sang.”

The admirer said the musician had sung Mohanam. The doubter objected: “No, no. He sang Mukhari.” The musician, deciding not to involve himself in this question of raga grammar, departed.

The critic then said: “Friends, both of you came very close to the truth. There is truth in saying he sang Mohanam and in saying he sang Mukhari. Because he did not properly understand the characteristics of either, he allowed them to mingle and turn into one another, and sang neither raga but a Mohana-Mukhari.”

Friends may ask where and when this occurred and who the four men were. It is merely a story, told to convey an idea.

Jayaprakash Narayan, an organiser and powerful figure of the Socialist Party, came to its Madurai conference and resounded through various cities of Tamil Nadu. A whirlwind tour, animated speeches and glittering publicity ensured that the public knew of them.

As far as possible, Congress newspapers published the Socialist leader's views. They did so even though his campaign aimed to create a countervailing force against the dominance of the Congress rulers whom those very papers vigorously supported.

It is astonishing. A hotel would not place at its entrance a bold sign reading, “Drinking coffee harms the body.” No doctor's house would display the sign “A specialist who never sees illness.” One cannot find a woman saying that powder is what makes her face shine. Yet we find Congress papers publishing a speaker's claims that Congress has decayed and is useless.

At first this wonder may bewilder many. Those whose thinking remains unstained will understand that there is nothing to marvel at, though there may be reason for fear. Even those who understand may not publicise it. Are there not many virtuous people who think, “Why should we announce what the country does not know and bring trouble upon ourselves?”

The wonder that papers labouring to protect and increase Congress strength published Jayaprakash's condemnation of Congress organisation and conduct contains a subtlety.

In some towns two cinemas have the same owner. If each had a different owner, one would not advertise the film shown at the other. But when both belong to one man, Vincent may carry an advertisement saying “*Kamavalli* at the Lighthouse,” while the Lighthouse advertises “*Veerayi* at Vincent.”

Congress-supporting papers seem similarly to have regarded Congress propaganda and Jayaprakash's socialist propaganda as two talking-picture halls belonging to one owner. Perhaps that is why they did not impose upon Jayaprakash the blackout used against other critics of Congress.

Until then Jayaprakash was treated as a favourite child. That is pleasing, but reflection upon the concealed subtlety burns away delight and makes fear sprout. What would one think if Churchill spoke in Berlin urging people to follow Stalin's path?

Yet *Dinasari*, *Dinamani*, *Mithran* and others decorate reports with headlines such as “Jayaprakash Visits” and “Welcome to Jayaprakash.” What is this marvel? How can its subtlety go unrecognised, and after recognising it, how can the truth remain unspoken?

With support from papers defending Congress, Jayaprakash toured and campaigned like a cyclone. Examine once and many times the ideas he gave Tamil Nadu. Observe the embrace and collision between one view and another, and the contradictions. The musician in our opening story will then appear before your mind's eye and the Mohana-Mukhari raga enter your ear.

Like a performer whose elaboration sounds at one moment like Mohanam and another like Mukhari, Jayaprakash's speeches carry a note of radicalism in one place and of Triplicane conservatism in another. In one section he appears fearless, tearing away the stories of Congress hypocrisy. In another, he seems to borrow Congress's own voice to sing. Thus this political musician, Vetri Oliyar, elaborates Mohana-Mukhari. Why? Friends who yearn for socialism must consider this important question.

Jayaprakash Narayan worked within Congress for more than twenty years and has now left it. He is a former Congress member, but the polish acquired there has not disappeared; naturally it will remain for some time.

He is not a Congressman—but he is not an enemy of Congress.

He is a socialist—but a strange socialist who refuses to join others demanding socialism.

He earnestly wants society's evils abolished, but does not assume a militant posture demanding the destruction of caste difference and the institutions built upon it. His eyes see a society afflicted by caste and he wishes that condition to change. Yet those who work to abolish caste through methods they consider right appear to him as communalists, and anger and agitation arise in his mind.

He himself says there is no use talking of ancient glory, yet summons Aurobindo and Ramana Rishi, preachers of the old, to lead the spiritual field.

He thunders that exploiters must be abolished while taking the pleasure-loving Prince of Vijayanagaram as his touring companion.

He says Congress government is improper, yet with the same mouth thunders that opposition must not be created against the present government.

He says Congress rule provides facilities for reactionaries and capitalists, yet declares trust and attachment toward Pandit Nehru, who leads that rule.

He says Congress began governing but brought no public welfare, yet in the same speech sternly warns that the deficiencies and errors of Congress rulers must not be used to build strength for a new party.

He says Congress merely promised that attaining power would allow it to create a comfortable life for people and, after beginning to rule, devised no plan to implement that promise. But he too merely promises that the Socialist Party will abolish exploitation and create well-being without announcing its plan.

Thus the ideas in his speeches are largely Mohana-Mukhari. What is his purpose? What lesson can people obtain from his views? What is the new path he lays? Emotionally committed young people who sincerely love the noble ideal of socialism must think about these matters. Calm reflection will remove the suspicion that we merely mock him and reveal that his discourse really is Mohana-Mukhari.

Consider some of his views:

“As under British rule, society still allows rich people on one side to conduct courts of splendour in multi-storeyed mansions and towering buildings, while on the other side...”

*[The quotation continues across the printed page break before the surviving text resumes its criticism of black-marketeers under Congress rule.]*

Did Pandit Nehru, with his international outlook, not know this condition would arise? Even if he failed to foresee it, does he not know now? Knowing it, what has he done? Why has he not moved even his little finger? Does he regard black-marketeers roaming in khadi shirts as perfectly ordinary or no great evil? Has his nature changed so far that he wants this condition? Or does he lack the power to alter it?

These questions cannot be brushed aside. Yet the leader founding a new party refuses even to consider them and sings a devotional hymn that Pandit Nehru is a very, very good man. If he is so good, how did all the wicked people Jayaprakash describes gain dominance within Congress before his eyes? Why does Jayaprakash refuse to answer?

Do the people need him to learn that Congress has become a camp of capitalists? Is the fact that Shanmukham Chetty drafted independent India's first budget not enough? People want to know why Congress changed in this way. How did Pandit Nehru—who once spoke a socialism far more appealing than Jayaprakash's present version—permit it? Why could he not develop the will to change it?

Jayaprakash discussed this economic problem like someone offering general instruction on goodness and justice. He did not explain it, offer an alternative programme or remove people's doubts. He sang Mohana-Mukhari and departed.

He spoke about social problems, like political and economic ones, without clarity or a plan.

“Our country has a graded caste system. Because each caste feels itself above or below another, one caste seeks to rule another. If the freedom we have obtained is to endure, caste madness must disappear.”

This appears in Jayaprakash's Tirunelveli speech. It is true, and we thank him for saying it. But what caused caste to arise? What are its evils? Why does it persist in this age of spreading knowledge? Without considering these, how can the full problem be understood? Why does he refuse to consider them?

He says caste madness must end, but what is the medicine? Great men and national leaders have said for more than forty years that it is wrong. The noble man praised as Father of the Nation also said so. Will a madness that survived all that now disappear by taking Jayaprakash's speech as medicine?

Why did this doctor not name the cure? He merely reported the disease and its pain, which people already know. If the Self-Respect potion is administered, its advocates are abused as atheists. If one says the madness will disappear by bathing in Dravidian oil because Dravidian culture contains the exalted philosophy that humanity is one community, they are shamelessly called communalists.

Is it enough for someone like Jayaprakash to mount a platform somewhere one day and sing rhythmically that caste madness is wrong? Will the madness leave? Its removal requires a great revolution in people's minds. Does Jayaprakash possess a programme for that intellectual revolution? His speeches show no indication.

Thus, without stating a way to transform politics, economics, society and the knowledge underlying them, he sings Mukhari by describing the condition and Mohanam by holding out hope that people's situation will improve—and departs.

We trust it is now clear why Congress newspapers published his views despite knowing he was campaigning in Hindi to form a party against Congress. They believe he does not touch fundamentals and therefore cannot endanger their dominance. If people angered by Congress conduct entered other parties that touch those fundamentals, the dominant would be endangered. Jayaprakash's Mohana-Mukhari method can prevent that danger. The papers therefore considered it safe to publicise his campaign. But how long can this remain possible?

## II. Dr Subbarayan Resigns

Madras Home Minister Dr P. Subbarayan resigned his office on 3 April 1948. In his statement, he said he was compelled to resign because the Premier interfered in his department and tried to conduct affairs without consulting him. The Premier stated in response that no such thing had occurred.

*Dravida Nadu*, 11 April 1948
